diagnosis_text:
A complete four-layer diagnostic pass found no fault in the network. Phase 1 reachability testing showed host_1_1_1_1 reaching the 10.200.0.x server farm with 0% packet loss. Phase A infrastructure sweep flagged zero devices — no ACL/firewall drop rules, no static ARP anomalies, no addressing or default-route problems on either host. The l2_snapshot reported only kernel tunnel pseudo-interface zero-MACs (tunl0, gre0, etc.), which are standard Linux baseline noise present on every device and carry no diagnostic weight. Phase B OSPF snapshot confirmed all six FRR routers are healthy: all daemons responsive via vtysh, every adjacency in Full state, and each router holds nine OSPF routes — the full topology-wide table. router_core_3 specifically showed three Full adjacencies and nine routes with no errors. Phase D service snapshot demonstrated that both hosts successfully resolve all four web hostnames (web0–web3.local) via the DNS server at 10.200.0.2 and receive HTTP 200 responses from each of the four web servers (10.200.0.3–10.200.0.6) in under 4 ms. All web servers also answered on localhost. Pressure sweep found no stress tools, CPU saturation, or socket exhaustion anywhere in the lab. There is no mechanism — no flap process, no OSPF instability trace, no ACL, no routing gap — that could produce the reported outage. The infrastructure is fully operational at the time of this investigation.

Root cause: No anomaly exists; the reported outage leaves no current evidence and no residual mechanism.

Fix: No corrective action required. If the issue recurs, capture a contemporaneous ping trace or syslog entry to provide evidence for a targeted re-investigation.
